---
import SiteLayout from '~/layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import MermaidDiagram from '~/components/MermaidDiagram.astro';
import { loadProjects } from '~/lib/manifest/load';
import { SITE } from '~/lib/site';
import { withBase } from '~/lib/urls';

const projects = loadProjects();
const active = projects.filter((project) => project.status !== 'archived');
---

<SiteLayout
  title={`About — ${SITE.fullName}`}
  description="About Purview Development, the ideas and principles behind the Purview-Dev .NET developer tooling family."
>
  <div class="pv-container py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="pv-chip mb-4 border-brand/30 bg-brand/5 text-brand">About</p>

      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight md:text-4xl">
        {SITE.orgName}
      </h1>

      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted">
        {SITE.tagline} Purview is where I build open-source tools focused on improving the .NET developer
        experience — removing repetitive work, catching problems earlier, and automating the things that
        shouldn't need continual attention.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_320px]">
      <div class="min-w-0 space-y-10">
        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Why Purview?</h2>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            I chose the name <strong class="font-medium text-default">Purview</strong>
            because I liked what it suggested about developer tooling.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            A purview is an area of influence or responsibility. That's a good description of what
            these projects represent: the parts of the development experience where I think better
            tooling can make a meaningful difference.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Purview isn't intended to be one enormous framework that dictates how an application
            should be built. It's a collection of focused tools for the areas within that purview —
            generation, validation, telemetry, event sourcing, .NET Aspire, build infrastructure,
            and the other repetitive pieces around building and maintaining software.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 font-medium">
            The common goal is simple: <strong>make development easier.</strong>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">From repetition to tooling</h2>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Most Purview projects are evolutions of something I originally built to solve a real
            problem.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            I'd build something for one project, encounter the same problem somewhere else, improve
            the solution, and eventually realise I was writing essentially the same thing again.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            At that point, it probably belongs in a library.
          </p>

          <MermaidDiagram
            label="The Purview process: solve a problem, encounter it again, generalise the solution, then automate it"
            caption="The pattern behind many Purview projects"
            diagram={`flowchart LR
              solve["Solve the\nproblem"] --> repeat["Encounter it\nagain"]
              repeat --> generalise["Generalise the\nsolution"]
              generalise --> automate["Automate it"]`}
          />

          <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            That thinking has become one of the principles behind Purview:
          </p>

          <blockquote class="mt-4 border-l-2 border-brand pl-4">
            <p class="text-lg font-semibold">Automation, not operation.</p>
          </blockquote>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            If something is predictable and repeatable, I'd rather automate it once than make
            developers remember to do it forever.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">The projects</h2>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Today, Purview spans {active.length} active projects covering areas such as event sourcing,
            strongly typed telemetry, schema validation, incremental source generation, .NET Aspire integrations,
            and shared build infrastructure.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Source generation features heavily because it fits the philosophy particularly well. If
            the compiler already has enough information to generate something correctly, I'd rather
            generate it than maintain repetitive runtime wiring or boilerplate by hand.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            It also moves more mistakes into the development cycle, where they are cheaper and
            easier to understand.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">How the projects fit together</h2>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Each project is intended to be useful independently. There isn't a requirement to adopt
            a "Purview stack".
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Where projects naturally complement each other, they share foundations and integrate
            with one another.
            <code>Purview.SourceGeneratorFramework</code> provides common infrastructure for source-generator-based
            projects, while
            <code>Purview.Build</code> and <code>Purview.BuildSdk</code> provide common build conventions
            and automation. Higher-level libraries can use other Purview components where doing so genuinely
            adds value.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The aim is to build
            <strong class="font-medium text-default">
              small, composable tools with consistent conventions
            </strong>, rather than a tightly coupled framework.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Use one because it solves a problem you have. Combine them where that makes sense.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Building in the open</h2>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Purview is also where I can take ideas that have grown out of day-to-day software
            development and explore them further.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Some projects are mature; others are experiments that are still evolving. Many are
            pre-1.0 while their APIs and approaches settle.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The projects share common tooling for building, testing, validation, packaging, and
            publishing. That infrastructure is itself part of Purview: if maintaining multiple
            projects creates repetitive work, that's another opportunity for automation.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            The same principle applies to maintaining the tools as it does to using them:
            <strong class="font-medium text-default"> automation, not operation. </strong>
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold">Open source</h2>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Purview's public projects are open source and released under the MIT licence.
          </p>

          <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
            Issues, ideas, and contributions are welcome. The projects are built around problems
            I've encountered, but the aim is for the resulting tools to be useful well beyond the
            projects that originally inspired them.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>

      <aside class="space-y-6">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-5">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">Organisation</h2>

          <dl class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm">
            <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class="text-muted">Home</dt>
              <dd class="text-right">
                <a href={SITE.url} class="pv-link">{SITE.domain}</a>
              </dd>
            </div>

            <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class="text-muted">GitHub</dt>
              <dd class="text-right">
                <a href={SITE.githubUrl} class="pv-link" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
                  purview-dev
                </a>
              </dd>
            </div>

            <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class="text-muted">Packages</dt>
              <dd class="text-right">
                <a
                  href={`${SITE.nugetUrl}/packages?q=purview+kieronlanning`}
                  class="pv-link"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  target="_blank"
                >
                  nuget.org
                </a>
              </dd>
            </div>

            <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
              <dt class="text-muted">Active projects</dt>
              <dd class="text-right">{active.length}</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-border bg-surface p-5">
          <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">Explore</h2>

          <ul class="mt-4 list-none space-y-2 text-sm">
            <li>
              <a href={withBase('/projects/')} class="pv-link">Projects</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={withBase('/docs/')} class="pv-link">Documentation</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={withBase('/releases/')} class="pv-link">Releases</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
